---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SqlLab from '../../components/SqlLab.tsx';
import data from '../../data/ch4-examples.json';
const pick = (id: string) => data.examples.find((e) => e.id === id)!;
const groupBy = pick('ch04-sql-a:1020');
const binderError = pick('ch04-sql-a:1051');
---
<Base title="SQL lab">
  <article>
    <h1>Lab: revenue per payment method</h1>
    <p>
      The text of the lesson is plain HTML: it is readable immediately, before any JavaScript runs. The SQL engine
      (DuckDB compiled to WebAssembly) only starts when the lab scrolls into view, in a Web Worker, with a progress bar.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run the query, then change it: try <code>count(distinct orderid)</code>, or remove the <code>where</code>. The
      check compares your result with the book (columns and values, order only when the query has an
      <code>order by</code>).
    </p>
    <SqlLab client:visible id="lab-group-by" title="Revenue per payment method (book 4.6)"
      initialSql={groupBy.sql} expectedRaw={groupBy.expectedRaw} ordered={groupBy.ordered} />
    <h2>When GROUP BY goes wrong</h2>
    <p>The book shows the exact error DuckDB prints. The checker accepts the error type and message.</p>
    <SqlLab client:visible id="lab-binder-error" title="Binder error (book 4.6)"
      initialSql={binderError.sql} expectedRaw={binderError.expectedRaw} ordered={binderError.ordered} />
  </article>
</Base>
